---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { M10_SCORES, RUBRIC_DIMENSIONS, THRESHOLD, PROVISIONAL_NOTE } from '../data/m10Scores.ts';

const REPO = 'https://github.com/Busy-Office-Codex/busy-office-ui';

// Until M10 (issue #24), this repo genuinely had no scoring/assessment/conformance system —
// confirmed by a repo-wide grep, a GitHub-org-wide search, and this exact question being the
// reason issue #21 (the website/content/sample-app reorganization this page is part of) exists
// at all. That's no longer true: M10 froze a real 9-dimension rubric and scored all 13 framework
// components against it (ROADMAP.md items 55, 59, 61-66). This page surfaces that score
// alongside what always existed here — the CI gate suite (below) and ROADMAP.md's own narrated
// review findings per milestone (linked, not duplicated — this page never restates a finding
// ROADMAP.md already owns).
const GATES = [
  { step: 'pnpm build', checks: 'The package builds: TS declarations, browser ESM, compiled StyleX CSS' },
  { step: 'pnpm lint', checks: 'ESLint across src, examples, preview, test' },
  { step: 'pnpm typecheck', checks: 'TypeScript, no emit, across 4 separate project configs (src/examples/preview/test)' },
  { step: 'pnpm test', checks: 'Unit + component tests (Vitest) — rendered-markup contracts, docs-contract enforcement, navigation-model validation, the theme-safety token-audit ratchet, real WCAG contrast math' },
  { step: 'pnpm security', checks: 'pnpm audit --prod --audit-level high' },
  { step: 'pnpm build:docs', checks: "docs-site builds against the real, just-built package — added by this workstream (issue #21) after finding docs-site's own build had been red on main for 29 consecutive pushes with nothing acting on it (this workflow didn't exist yet)" },
  { step: 'node docs-site/scripts/check-links.mjs', checks: 'Every internal link in the built docs-site resolves, case-exact (fragment/#anchor links are not checked)' },
  { step: 'node docs-site/scripts/check-live-demos.mjs', checks: "Every doc page's live demo actually rendered — not a silent runtime error swallowed into a visible-but-unnoticed <pre role=\"alert\"> box" },
  { step: 'node docs-site/scripts/check-llms-txt.mjs', checks: 'The generated /llms.txt lists every real component doc, and is non-empty' },
  { step: 'pnpm test:browser', checks: 'Playwright, real browser — a dedicated spec file for most sample screens, the rest covered by cross-cutting suites (accessibility/contrast, focus/keyboard, layout/responsiveness, navigation, dark-mode)' },
];

const sortedScores = [...M10_SCORES].sort((a, b) => b.score - a.score);
---
<Base title="Quality & Verification">
  <a href="/">&larr; Home</a>
  <h1>Quality &amp; Verification</h1>
  <p>What actually verifies a change here, and what it doesn't. Two things exist: a CI gate suite that runs on every push to <code>main</code> (below), and a real per-component review score from M10's framework-scoring round (<a href={`${REPO}/issues/24`}>issue #24</a>) — the first, and so far only, scoring system this repo has ever had. A narrated, per-milestone review record is kept in <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/ROADMAP.md`}><code>ROADMAP.md</code></a> — this page points at it rather than duplicating it.</p>

  <h2>Component scores (M10, issue #24)</h2>
  <p>Every framework component in <code>src/components/*.tsx</code> as of M10 (13 of the 14 files there today — <code>Avatar</code> was added afterward, by <a href={`${REPO}/issues/25`}>issue #25</a>, and hasn't been scored under this rubric yet) was scored 0-4 on each of <strong>{RUBRIC_DIMENSIONS.length} dimensions</strong> — {RUBRIC_DIMENSIONS.join(', ').toLowerCase()} — summed and normalized to a score out of 100. A target had to clear a <strong>{THRESHOLD}/100</strong> threshold, with every applicable dimension at or above its own floor (the score is capped by the weakest dimension, never averaged away). Full dimension definitions live in issue #24; the numbers below are hand-transcribed from <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/ROADMAP.md`}><code>ROADMAP.md</code></a>'s own item text (there's no structured score store to query live) — each row cites the item it came from so it can be checked against the source.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Score</th><th>Clears {THRESHOLD}?</th><th>Clears every floor?</th><th>ROADMAP item</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {sortedScores.map((row) => (
        <tr>
          <td><a href={`/components/${row.id}/`}>{row.id}</a></td>
          <td>{row.score}/100</td>
          <td>{row.score >= THRESHOLD ? 'Yes' : 'No'}</td>
          <td>{row.clearsFloor ? 'Yes' : 'No'}</td>
          <td>{row.item}</td>
          <td>{row.note ?? '—'}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p><strong>{PROVISIONAL_NOTE}</strong> Every score above is the final, post-independent-review number — the round's own summary discloses that review caught and fixed a real defect (a score-arithmetic error, an incomplete-scope claim, two overclaims in one item) in close to every batch it shipped, so the first-drafted numbers were not what shipped.</p>

  <h2>The gate suite</h2>
  <p>Every one of these runs on every push to <code>main</code> (<a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/.github/workflows/gates.yml`}><code>.github/workflows/gates.yml</code></a>) — a red run is fixed before any other work, per <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/AGENTS.md`}><code>AGENTS.md</code></a>. Run the same commands locally, in this order, before handing off a change:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>What it checks</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {GATES.map((gate) => (
        <tr>
          <td><code>{gate.step}</code></td>
          <td>{gate.checks}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>The full local sequence, including the two setup/fresh-checkout steps not listed above (<code>pnpm install --frozen-lockfile</code>, the Playwright browser install) plus <code>pnpm build:preview</code> — required by <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/AGENTS.md`}><code>AGENTS.md</code></a>'s own handoff list even though it isn't a `gates.yml` step — is <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/README.md#local-checks`}>README.md's "Local checks"</a>.</p>
  <p>See it actually running: <a href={`${REPO}/actions/workflows/gates.yml`}>live run history</a>. A separate workflow, <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/.github/workflows/docs.yml`}><code>docs.yml</code></a>, rebuilds and publishes this documentation site as a container image on every push to <code>main</code> touching <code>docs/</code>, <code>docs-site/</code>, <code>src/</code> or <code>examples/</code> — it duplicates the build+check-links steps deliberately (a publish job, not a merge gate) — see its own <a href={`${REPO}/actions/workflows/docs.yml`}>run history</a>.</p>

  <h2>Review process</h2>
  <p>Every batch of changes gets an acceptance-to-test mapping and an independent, fresh-context reviewer before merge (never the same session that wrote the change) — required by <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/AGENTS.md`}><code>AGENTS.md</code></a>/<a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/LOOP.md`}><code>LOOP.md</code></a>. A milestone-closing batch gets three parallel review lenses instead of one (spec-match, contrast/accessibility, simplicity) — a clause fails if any lens fails it. The actual findings from every review — what was checked, what was found, what was fixed before merge — are narrated in <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/ROADMAP.md`}><code>ROADMAP.md</code></a> itself, item by item, not restated here: read the source, not a summary of it.</p>

  <h2>Known limitations</h2>
  <p>Disclosed, not silently dropped — each traces to its own <code>ROADMAP.md</code> item for the full context:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Structural-first-pass, not pixel-verified.</strong> Every M6 sample screen (items 17–33) matches the ERP skeleton reference's own labels and composition, but wasn't independently pixel-measured or contrast-audited screen-by-screen the way item 12's own fidelity pass did — that remains open.</li>
    <li><strong>Shell's own chrome doesn't re-tint.</strong> The command bar, dock and palette (item 36, item 39) stay their original theme even under an explicit <code>Theme</code> override — a viewer who picks "Dark" sees dark page content inside permanently-light chrome.</li>
    <li><strong>Two colors have no token, by design.</strong> <code>Launcher.tsx</code>'s favorited-star amber and <code>entryScreenLayout.tsx</code>'s local "warn" amber (item 49) — two different shades for two different one-off purposes, not a proven shared concept worth a token yet. Every other raw color literal in <code>examples/</code> is gone, mechanically enforced by <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/test/shell-token-audit.test.ts`}><code>test/shell-token-audit.test.ts</code></a>'s ratchet across all 47 files. Spacing has no equivalent ratchet yet.</li>
    <li><strong>Route-to-route navigation between sample screens is out of scope, not the in-page handoffs.</strong> Several M7 screens already hand off to each other live — <code>Billing.tsx</code>'s "View invoice" action opens the shared-store record on <code>Invoice.tsx</code>, <code>Analytics.tsx</code>'s exceptions focus a record on the target screen, <code>Requisitions.tsx</code> carries a linked-purchase-order breadcrumb — see <code>examples/README.md</code>'s own "named journeys" section. What's genuinely out of scope is real <em>route</em>-to-route navigation (list → detail → edit → confirmation as separate URLs): ROADMAP.md's own words, "explicitly OUT of this scope — it would be new interactive behavior in <code>examples/</code>, not a content reorg, and needs its own proposal."</li>
    <li><strong><code>Chart</code>'s <code>'pie'</code> variant is a disclosed, not-agreed proposal.</strong> Item 34's 5 originally named consumers (BI dashboard, Inventory, Reports, Finance, BI explore) all landed; issue #16's own "Pie" ask for BI explore's chart toggle did not — this repo's own Objective 1/3 ("refuse a variant with one caller" / "a new shared component needs two named consumers") apply, since nothing else asks for a pie chart. Proposed on <a href={`${REPO}/issues/16`}>issue #16</a> rather than force-added or silently dropped; BI explore renders its pivot as <code>'bar'</code> instead.</li>
    <li><strong>Several of <code>examples/</code>'s Chart- and KPI-backed "this month"/"this year" figures are undisclosed-in-the-UI sample data.</strong> <code>Dashboard.tsx</code>'s revenue trend, "Revenue by region" bar and "Revenue mix" donut (all three read from the same hand-written <code>REVENUE_TREND</code>/<code>REVENUE_BY_REGION</code>/<code>REVENUE_MIX</code> literals, not a live store aggregation — the store has no per-region/per-channel revenue breakdown to aggregate from); <code>BuilderReports.tsx</code>'s own "Revenue this month" KPI tile and "Revenue trend" widget (the same $486K/6-month-trend literals as <code>Dashboard.tsx</code>'s own KPI card and chart, copied by hand, not read live); and <code>Finance.tsx</code>'s "Cash flow, last 12 months". Disclosed in each file's own header comment, but a viewer looking only at the running app has no visual cue distinguishing them from the real, live figures on the same screens (<code>Finance.tsx</code>'s "Receivables"; <code>BuilderReports.tsx</code>'s bar/donut/table widgets, which DO read live <code>stockLevels</code>/<code>requisitions</code>; <code>BiExplore.tsx</code>'s pivot).</li>
    <li><strong>Docs-site is v1 scope</strong> (item 15): no search, no versioning, no build-time check beyond the link checker.</li>
  </ul>
</Base>
